  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "uSOgbVp+C8639vvc0p7oz8cULMA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:35:43.974Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The global/local split is not a partition of one log \u2014 it is two logs. Each scope has its own state shape, its own operation sequence, and its own indices:\n\n```ts\ndoc.state.global;      // shared with everyone\ndoc.state.local;       // yours alone\n\ndoc.operations.global; // replicated log\ndoc.operations.local;  // local-only log\n```\n\nThe operation at index 5 in `global` has **nothing to do** with index 5 in `local`. The two counters advance independently, so you cannot interleave them, compare them, or use one as a proxy for document age. There is no global ordering across the pair, and none is needed: the Reactor's sync engine replicates `global` across peers and leaves `local` completely alone.\n\nThe practical consequences:\n\n- **Indexing is per scope.** When asserting on an operation in a test, the index counts dispatches *into that scope*, not total dispatches against the document. A document with 3 local operations and 1 global operation has the global one at `operations.global[0]`.\n- **A local dispatch is a dead end by design.** Updating your \"show completed\" filter appends to the local log, runs the local reducer, and stops. Teammates never see it \u2014 no sync traffic, no operation for peers to replay.\n- **Revisions track separately.** Per-scope revision tracking follows from per-scope logs; there is no single document revision number spanning both.\n\nThis is also why moving a field between scopes is a schema change rather than a config toggle: you are moving it between two different histories.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/03-document-models/02-state-and-scopes.mdx (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:35:43.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:35:43.974Z"
    }
  },
